  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe impressive stretch of dry weather and near normal\ntemperatures continues today and will last for the early part of\nthe work week as the Omega Block gradually breaks down. A\nnorthern stream mid/upper shortwave trough, currently over the\nnorthern Great Lakes, will slide through Ontario, Quebec, and\nthe eastern Great Lakes Monday but will quickly deamplify due to\nstrengthening quasi-zonal flow across the CONUS. This will allow\nthe large surface high to retreat southwest from its position\nalong the East Coast this afternoon into the Tennessee Valley\nMonday, which will allow a weak cold front on the western edge\nof the shortwave to drop in from the north Monday afternoon.\nThis front will wash out over the CWA due to the mid/upper flow\nrapidly turning quasi-zonal, but strong Canadian high pressure\nat the surface building in behind it across the Great Lakes\nMonday night and Tuesday will advect noticeably cooler air into\nthe region, especially near the lakeshore given gusty NE winds.\nExpect highs Tuesday to fall into the upper 50s/low 60s near \nthe lakeshore, with mid/upper 60s further south, which will be\ncooler compared to Monday. \n\nThe developing quasi-zonal flow pattern mentioned above will be\nin full force by Wednesday and will continue into next weekend\nas a subtropical Pacific jet extension, a response to the\npowerful El Nino, pumps extensive shortwave energy into the West\nCoast. This will eventually carve out a mid/upper trough and\nassociated large closed low along the West Coast next weekend,\nhelping to build a broad mid/upper ridge over the eastern CONUS.\nAs this all occurs, the Canadian surface high over the Great\nLakes Tuesday will retreat off the New England coast the second\nhalf of the week, supporting deepening southerly return flow.\nThe end result will be a warming trend Wednesday into next\nweekend, with temperatures 10 degrees above normal at times.\nThis will also allow the dry weather to continue until a\npotential cold front late Saturday into Sunday, however, there\ncould be a few showers Thursday night in far NE Ohio and NW PA\nas a northern stream shortwave and weak cold front try to brush\nthe region. Any QPF would be very light until a more widespread\nchance for rain late Saturday into Sunday depending on phasing \nbetween northern and southern stream energy.",
